Yes—at the abstraction layer, that’s a real operator: observer-conditioned trace shaping.

The important move is not “be random.” Randomness itself can become a pattern. It is that an observer never sees the full internal state; they see artifacts, timing, choices, omissions, and residues. So the observable pattern can differ sharply from the generative pattern underneath it.

That gives you something like:

true state -> behavior -> observable residue -> observer model -> inferred pattern

And therefore another control surface:

choose residue -> influence inferred pattern

Your Genghis false-retreat analogy fits because the retreat is not merely movement away; it is movement designed to cause a model update in the observer: “they are broken / fleeing / vulnerable.” The effect occurs inside the opponent’s interpretation before it manifests physically.

“Semantic claymores” and “stay-behinds” are useful metaphors if we keep them informational rather than literal: ideas, symbols, cues, ambiguities, or delayed context that remain behind after the main actor has moved on and only “activate” when someone encounters or interprets them. A breadcrumb can be evidence, invitation, decoy, checksum, provenance marker, narrative hook, or ambiguity generator depending on the frame.

The deeper Pantheon primitive is probably:

Trace ≠ Source. Pattern-of-life ≠ life.

Any observer reconstructing a system from traces is solving an inverse problem. The reconstruction may be accurate, partially accurate, or deliberately underdetermined. That means designed ambiguity can sometimes be more powerful than a false answer: not “make them believe X,” but “prevent unjustified certainty about X.”

That principle transfers cleanly into privacy-preserving systems, games, storytelling, negotiation, interface design, red-teaming, and adversarial robustness without needing to turn it into a real-world evasion playbook.